# Tab entities: URLs, breadcrumbs, page titles

Every entity that opens in an editor tab (console, dashboard, table, app,
file, data source, flow, connector, settings section, plan, ...) MUST have:

1. **A unique, shareable URL** that the address bar shows while the tab is
   focused, and that restores the tab when opened directly (deep link).
2. **A breadcrumb bar** rendered with the shared `EntityBreadcrumbs`
   component, with a trail that starts at the workspace root and mirrors the
   sidebar: `<workspace name> / <sidebar section> / <entity path>` (e.g.
   `Acme / Databases / prod / public / users`).
3. **A page title** — `document.title` is derived from the active tab's
   `title` in `Editor.tsx`, so the tab title must be kept accurate (use
   `consoleStore.updateTitle` once the entity loads if the tab was opened
   from a deep link with a placeholder title).

## Compile-time guards (do not weaken)

- [app/src/lib/tab-routing.ts](mdc:app/src/lib/tab-routing.ts) —
  `tabUrlPath()` (tab → URL) and `TAB_DEEP_LINK_PATTERNS` (URL → kind) are
  exhaustive over `TabKind`. Adding a kind to `TabKind` without deciding its
  URL fails `tsc`.
- [app/src/components/EntityBreadcrumbs.tsx](mdc:app/src/components/EntityBreadcrumbs.tsx)
  — `segmentsForTab()` is exhaustive over `TabKind`; a new kind without a
  breadcrumb trail fails `tsc`.
- [app/src/lib/tab-routing.test.ts](mdc:app/src/lib/tab-routing.test.ts) —
  requires a fixture per kind and verifies each generated URL is matched by
  its own deep-link pattern (round-trip). Run with
  `pnpm --filter app run test:unit`.

Never "fix" a compile error from these guards by adding a `default` case,
casting, or returning `null` without a deliberate decision. `null` is only
acceptable for kinds that genuinely cannot be deep-linked (currently only
the legacy `members` kind).

## Sidebar highlight + reveal (do not weaken)

Every entity that opens in a tab and has a sidebar explorer row MUST:

1. **Highlight its row when focused.** The explorer passes `activeItemId` to
   `ResourceTree`. `ResourceTree` decides the highlight via the single helper
   [isSidebarRowActive](mdc:app/src/components/resource-tree/utils.ts) and
   applies it to **both file and folder rows**. Apps and Postgres tables open
   from a *directory* row, so folder rows must respect `activeItemId` too — a
   past regression highlighted files but not folders, leaving open apps
   un-highlighted. Guarded by `resource-tree/utils.test.ts` (a structural test
   asserts folder rows use `isSelectedLocation || isActive`).
2. **Be reachable by reveal.** [explorer-reveal.ts](mdc:app/src/lib/explorer-reveal.ts)
   maps a tab → `{ explorer, nodeId }`, where `nodeId` MUST equal the id the
   owning explorer builds for that `ResourceTree` node. The switch is
   exhaustive over `TabKind` (return `null` for kinds with no sidebar home).
   `tabUrlPath`/breadcrumbs and this mapping must agree on the owning explorer.

Reveal flow: producers (the tab-switch effect in `App.tsx`, clickable
breadcrumbs in `EntityBreadcrumbs.tsx`) call
`useExplorerRevealStore.requestReveal(explorer, nodeId)`. Each explorer
subscribes with `selectRevealFor(<explorer>)` and threads `revealNodeId` /
`revealNonce` into its `ResourceTree`, which expands ancestors and scrolls the
row into view. Tab-switch auto-scroll only fires when the owning explorer is
already on screen; breadcrumb clicks also switch the explorer first.

When adding a new tab kind with a sidebar row, add its case to
`tabRevealTarget`, make sure the explorer passes `activeItemId` +
`revealNodeId`/`revealNonce`, and (if it uses encoded node ids like apps)
source the separators from `explorer-reveal.ts` so reveal ids never drift.

## Checklist for adding a new tab kind

1. Add the kind to `TabKind` in
   [app/src/store/lib/types.ts](mdc:app/src/store/lib/types.ts). Typecheck
   now fails in the guarded files until steps 2–3 are done.
2. `lib/tab-routing.ts`: add the URL builder case in `tabUrlPath` and the
   matching regex in `TAB_DEEP_LINK_PATTERNS`; add the hydration handler in
   `UrlSync.tsx` (match most-specific routes first; dedupe against existing
   tabs before opening a new one). Add the fixture in `tab-routing.test.ts`.
3. `EntityBreadcrumbs.tsx`: add the `segmentsForTab` case. Trail starts at
   the workspace root, then the sidebar section label, then the entity path.
4. `Editor.tsx`: add the tab icon and the content renderer. The breadcrumb
   bar is rendered centrally by `Editor.tsx`; only add the kind to its
   `rendersOwnBreadcrumbs` exclusion list if the view embeds
   `EntityBreadcrumbs` itself (needed only to place actions in the bar via
   the `trailing` prop, like the table refresh button).
5. Add an open-or-focus helper following the `focusAppTab` /
   `focusDashboardTab` pattern (`app/src/app-runtime/shell.ts`,
   `app/src/dashboard-runtime/shell.ts`) so the tab is deduped everywhere.
6. Keep the tab `title` synced once the entity loads (placeholder titles
   come from deep-link hydration).
